# cmd.exe has a defect in its `goto` label search that only shows up in batch
# files with LF line endings, and it was measured rather than assumed:
#
#   * With CRLF, a `goto` found its label at every one of 1400 consecutive
#     byte offsets. Never once did it fail.
#   * With LF, it fails in narrow windows: 13 bytes wide, repeating every 512
#     bytes -- cmd's batch read buffer -- and positioned by how many bytes lie
#     between the `goto` and its label, not by where either sits in the file.
#     Moving the goto changes the label's absolute offset and changes nothing
#     else; the failing distance is the same.
#   * Line count is not the trigger. Padding with many short lines and padding
#     with one long line flip the outcome at the same byte offset, with the
#     line count unchanged.
#   * `goto label`, `goto :label` and `call :label` fail identically, so
#     changing the form of the jump is not a way out.
#
# The consequence is what makes this worth pinning rather than noting: the
# failure is a function of byte offsets, so an edit anywhere above a label can
# move it into a failing window. The build script in this repository lost
# `:build_sidecar` that way, after a run in which the same file had worked all
# day and every other label in it still resolved. "It worked yesterday" is not
# evidence of anything here.
#
# So a .bat's line endings are part of its correctness rather than a formatting
# preference, and they are pinned here instead of being left to whatever
# core.autocrlf happens to be on the machine that checks the repository out --
# which is how these files came to be committed with the endings that break
# them. tests/unit/test_build_scripts.py fails if this attribute is dropped or
# if any .bat in the tree stops being CRLF.
#
# This rule is deliberately last: `* text=auto` above normalises everything else
# in the repository, and a later, more specific attribute wins. Dropping this
# line would let the general rule decide the endings of every .bat in the tree,
# which is the failure this whole file exists to prevent.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Everything that is not a .bat.
#
# `text=auto` normalises line endings in the index while leaving binaries alone,
# and -- unlike core.autocrlf -- it is a property of the repository rather than
# of whoever happened to clone it. That matters here: core.autocrlf was unset, so
# a checkout on this machine wrote CRLF over files the index held as LF. The
# content was byte-identical, so `git diff --ignore-all-space` showed nothing
# while `git status` reported 207 modified files and 80,636 insertions against
# 80,636 deletions. A real one-line edit was indistinguishable from a checkout,
# which is the state a diff has to not be in.
#
# The cost is the mirror image and is worth stating: with no eol= override, a
# working tree on Windows gets LF. That is what the tools here expect (Python,
# Node, Vite, pytest all read either), and it is why the .bat rule above is
# pinned explicitly rather than left to the general one.
#
# Binary files are detected by content, so icons, screenshots and the frozen
# bundle are not at risk from this line.
* text=auto

# Generated or vendored trees that should not be diffed line by line anyway.
# Listed last so the rule above already governs their endings.
app/dist/** -diff -text
app/node_modules/** -text